    "result_text": "Build is green \u2014 `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0`. The audit log confirms the cap works: owner id=11 (alone in its household \u2192 uncapped) got `membershipLevel=1`, and id=12 (household max was 1, would score to level 2) got `membershipLevel=2` = 1+1.\n\n## What I changed\n\nThe spec is the membership-level cap, but the new test `coreCapsLevelByHousehold` creates *two* owners in one household (same last name + postcode, distinct telephones) and requires both creates to succeed. The endpoint's duplicate block was rejecting the second with a 409, so two things were needed. I kept each piece of logic in its natural home:\n\n1. **`Household.maxMembershipLevel(householdId, owners)`** \u2014 a new query over household members, alongside the existing `size(...)`, returning `OptionalInt.empty()` when the household has no member (so \"no cap applies\" is expressible).\n\n2. **`MembershipPoints.capToHousehold(level, householdMax)`** \u2014 the arithmetic rule (\"at most one above\"), with the `1` as a named constant, next to the other level rules it owns.\n\n3. **`AssignMembershipLevel`** \u2014 orchestration only: computes the level, then applies the cap when a household member exists. Also folded the two `findAll()` calls into one.\n\n4. **`EnsureUniqueIdentity`** \u2014 retargeted the duplicate block from *household* collision to *exact-identity* collision, reusing the existing `IdentityKey` (the class already documented as \"the value that decides when two owners are the same person\"). Genuine additional household members with a distinct telephone/email are now admitted; true duplicates still 409, and the `sharesHousehold` opt-in and the \"no household \u2192 nothing to collide with\" short-circuit are preserved.\n\nI updated the now-stale javadocs on `EnsureUniqueIdentity`, `DuplicateIdentityException`, `FlagPossibleDuplicate`, and `IdentityKey` (and the exception message) to match, so the docs stay truthful about the new keying.\n\nOne judgment call worth flagging: the task text described only the cap, but the supplied test can't pass while the household-level block stands, and the test's explicit \"unique telephone\" note points at identity-based deduplication \u2014 so I made that the minimal, reuse-oriented behavior change rather than editing the test.",
